From: CarPlay C0 lane (2026-11-21)
Subject: 0509 sweet_nub: anchor the controller vtable on the instance, not on a link-time address

0508 validated and copied the 232-slot IOUSBDeviceController vtable from the constant
0xfffffff005e11210 and required each of the 18 pure slots to hold a kernel-range pointer.
Before spending a boot on that, tools/carplay/check_nub_prelink.py mapped the prelinks
through LC_SEGMENT_64 and compared them:

  prelink                  vtable VA            slot 0         slot 86        pure target
  0507+accessory (record)  0xfffffff005e11210  0xfffffff004cee5bc  0xfffffff004cef59c  0xfffffff0079eff24
  0508-acckeys (S3 image)  same                 same           same           0xfffffff0079f0394
  0508b-acckeys (rebuilt)  same                 same           same           0xfffffff0079f0394

The kext-region pointers are therefore stable across kernel changes, while the pure-virtual
target lives in __TEXT and moves with it. 0508's check only tested the range, so it tolerated
that by luck: it was one prelink away from refusing a good image, and it could not distinguish
"the vtable moved" from "the kernel moved".

The object's first word is the vtable its constructor installed, and it is the table every
later dispatch goes through. 0509 reads that pointer from the allocated instance, logs it
against the constant, validates only layout-independent invariants (table in the kernel image,
start slot in kernel text, all 18 pure slots identical) and copies from the live table. A moved
constant is reported, never fatal -- so a prelink rebuild can no longer waste a boot.

Verification: tools/carplay/check_nub_prelink.py reports constants_current=true for both
0508-acckeys and 0508b-acckeys (start slot and pure target recorded); the patch applies cleanly
to the file that 0508 creates.

